e'll come tonight at nine o'clock and have us invite Kidd over. Other people also came, all invited by Kidd.\" [3157] \"Quite a gathering,\" Bill said, looking around. \"The French detective sitting by the window, the British detective by the fireplace. Such a rich mix of nationalities. Where's the representative of the Stars and Stripes?\" [3158] Bender shook his head. [3159] \"Mr. Fischer has disappeared without a trace. Virginia is also missing. But everyone else is here. Bill, I have a feeling that the moment of truth is about to arrive, and soon someone will shout, 'It's James, the butler!' We're just waiting for Kidd to arrive.\" [3160] \"He won't show up,\" Bill said. [3161] \"Then why call this a 'company meeting'? That's what my father named it.\" [3162] \"There's definitely more to it. He obviously wants us to gather here, and he's probably somewhere else, you know the kind of trick he likes to pull.\" [3163] \"You mean he won't come?\" [3164] \"Of course not. Would he still walk into a lion's den? This meeting hall is filled with detectives and high-ranking officials.\" [3165] \"If you think so, then you don't know Victor King well. It's said that this is his favorite scene, and he always manages to escape unscathed.\" [3166] Eversley shakes his head skeptically. [3167] \"This is against all odds. He won't\u2026\"\n[3168] The door opens, and Tredwell announces:\n[3169] \"Mr. Cade.\" [3170] Anthony strides towards the host. [3171] \"Lord Carterham.\" He says, \"I do apologize for the inconvenience, but I believe that tonight, all secrets will be revealed.\" [3172] Lord Carterham seems relieved, and he has always had an inexplicable fondness for Anthony. [3173] \"No trouble at all.\" He says sincerely. [3174] \"You are too kind.\" Anthony says, \"Everyone is here, so I will begin.\" [3175] \"I don't understand,\" George Romax pretends to be confused, \"I don't understand. This is irregular. What is Mr. Kidd's status? The situation is delicate and complicated. I strongly suggest\u2026\"\n[3176] George's eloquent speech was interrupted by the Sheriff quietly walking over to him and whispering something. After a moment, George suddenly looked puzzled. [3177] \"Well, if you say so.\" He said reluctantly. Then, he loudly added, \"Let's hear what he has to say.\" [3178] Anthony did not let the obvious contempt bother him. [3179] \"It's just a small idea, that's all.\" He said cheerfully. \"As you all know, we received a cipher a few days ago that mentioned Richmond and some numbers. We attempted to decrypt it, but failed. Now, using the late Count Stapleton's memoirs\u2014which I happened to read\u2014we can solve this puzzle. The memoirs mentioned a dinner party with a 'flower' theme, where all the guests wore floral symbols. The Count's own badge, which we found in the strange emblem on the wall of the secret passage, represents a rose. You should remember the rows of things: buttons, the letter E, and weaving. Now, what in this villa has rows? Books, right? And in the Earl of Carrington's library catalog, there is a book titled 'The Earl of Richmond.' Now you all know where the hidden jewels are. From that book, using those numbers to search the bookshelves and book numbers, our long-sought item should be hidden in a fake book or a cave behind a book.\" [3180] Anthony modestly looked around, clearly waiting for applause from the audience. [3181] \"That's too clever!\" said the Earl of Carringham. [3182] \"Very clever,\" George modestly admitted. \"But the facts still need to be confirmed.\" [3183] Anthony laughed loudly. [3184] \"Practice makes perfect, right? I'll show you.\" He jumped up. \"I'll go to the study\u2026\"\n[3185] Before he could take another step, Mr. Lemon walked over from the window. [3186] \"Wait a moment, Mr. Kidd. Marquis of Carringham, if I may?\" [3187] He walked over to the desk, hastily scribbled a few lines, put them in an envelope, and rang the bell. [3188] Tredwell rushed in. [3189] \"Please deliver this immediately,\" said Lemon. [3190] \"Yes, sir,\" said Tredwell. [3191] He withdrew with his usual dignity. [3192] Anthony, who had been standing there hesitantly, sat down again. [3193] \"What do you think, Lermontov?\" he asked mildly. [3194] Suddenly, the air was filled with a tense atmosphere. [3195] \"If the diamond is where you say it is, it has been hidden for over seven years, so it doesn't matter if it's hidden for a little longer.\" [3196] \"Go on,\" said Anthony. \"You're not finished yet.\" [3197] \"Yes, I'm not finished. It would be unwise to let anyone leave the room at this point, especially the person who is unknown to us.\" [3198] Anthony raised his eyebrows and lit a cigarette. [3199] \"I think a life of wandering is not very respectable,\" he pondered. [3200] \"Mr. Kidd, two months ago, you were in South Africa. That is undeniable. And before that, where were you?\" [3201] Anthony leaned back in his chair, puffing on his cigarette rings. [3202] \"Canada, the wilds of the northwest.\" [3203] \"Are you sure you haven't been in jail? French jail?\" [3204] Detective Baudet took a step towards the door, as if to block the exit. But Anthony showed no sign of retaliation. [3205] Instead, he looked at the French detective and burst out laughing. [3206] \"Poor Lemont, you're obsessed! You see everyone as the Duke of Vicor. So, you think I'm the one in the spotlight?\" [3207] \"Do you deny it?\" [3208] Anthony brushed the ash off his sleeve. [3209] \"I never deny anything that's interesting.\" He said casually, \"But this accusation is too ridiculous.\" [3210] \"You think so?\" The Frenchman leaned forward, his face contorted in pain, looking perplexed as if Anthony's response had left him bewildered. [3211] \"Sir, if I tell you that this time, I'm here to arrest Victor King, nothing can stop me. What will you do?\" [3212] \"Very impressive,\" Anthony commented, \"you've come out before to arrest him, haven't you, Lemond? He's more cunning than yo