e poor fellow's been having a rough time lately, his head's been beaten pretty badly. I've been taking care of him.\" [3242] Suddenly, Mr. Issakov's deep voice came from the side: \"Can we guess who it is?\" [3243] \"Be my guest.\" Anthony said, \"But -\"\n[3244] Mr. Lemont interrupted him angrily:\n[3245] \"All nonsense! You think you're smarter than me. What you say might be true, you weren't in America at the time. If it weren't true, you wouldn't have said it. But there's another thing, a murder! Yes, a murder! Prince Michael's murder. That night when you were looking for the diamond, he ruined your scheme.\" [3246] \"Lemaire, have you heard of Victor King killing anyone?\" Anthony's voice was sharp. \"You know better than me, he never kills.\" [3247] \"Who else would kill him?\" Lemaire shouted. \"Tell me!\" [3248] As he finished speaking, a sharp whistle came from the corridor. Anthony put aside his previous indifference and jumped up. [3249] \"Didn't you ask me who killed Michael Prince?\" he called. \"I don't need to tell you, I'll show you. That whistle is the signal I've been waiting for. The murderer of Michael is now in the library.\" [3250] He jumped out of the window, followed by the others, circling the platform, and coming to the window of the library. He pushed it, and the window opened. [3251] He gently pulled the thick window curtain aside, and everyone could see the scene inside the room. [3252] A shadow stood in front of the bookshelf, intently pulling out one book after another and putting them back, completely oblivious to the noise outside. [3253] The figure held a flashlight, the dim light of which vaguely outlined his silhouette, and everyone stood there trying to recognize his appearance. Suddenly, someone jumped past them, letting out a roar like a wild beast. [3254] The flashlight fell to the ground and went out, and the room was filled with the sounds of terrifying struggle. The Earl of Carrington felt his way to the switch and turned on the light. [3255] Two people were wrestling, and when the crowd surrounded them, everything was over. A quick gunshot, and the short man bent over and fell to the ground. The other turned around to face everyone, it was Percy. His eyes were filled with anger. [3256] \"She killed my master,\" he roared, \"and now she wants to kill me. I took the gun from her and pointed it at her, but it went off during the struggle. It was the orders of the Duke of Michael, and this evil woman is dead.\" [3257] \"A woman?\" George Romax exclaimed. [3258] They approached the body, which lay on the floor, holding the gun in her hand, with a sinister expression on her face. It was Miss White. [3259] * * *\n[3260] [1] Alphonse Bertillon: A French Parisian clerk in the Identification Department in the 19th century; he developed the first modern system of criminal identification. This system, known as \"Bertillonage,\" consists of three parts: physical measurements, accurate descriptions of the physical characteristics of the suspect, and standardized facial photographs. [3261] Chapter 28: The Prince Victor\n[3262] \"I suspected her from the beginning,\" Anthony said, \"There was light in her room on the night of the murder. Later, I was unsure. I went to Bridesdale to investigate her, and when I returned, I believed she was Miss White. I was stupid at the time. Because Countess Bridesdale did indeed employ a Miss White and praised her highly. I never thought that the real Miss White was kidnapped on her way to her job and replaced by someone else. I even suspected Mr. Fiche. It was only when he came to Dover with me that we finally cleared the air. Then I began to see what was really going on. When I realized that he was from Pinkerton's and was tracking Prince Victor, my suspicions fell back on the original person.\" [3263] \"What most troubled me was that Mrs. Ruef recognized the woman, and it was only after I mentioned that the woman was the governess of Countess Bridesdale that Mrs. Ruef recognized her, and she only said that she felt the woman was familiar. Inspector Barton will soon explain to everyone that there was a carefully planned conspiracy to prevent Mrs. Ruef from coming to the Smokehouse. It was just a dead body after all. Although the person was killed by the Red Hand for betrayal, the planning of the entire action, as well as the absence of the Red Hand's mark, shows that there must have been a mastermind behind the scenes. At first, I thought it had something to do with Hesslov, as Mrs. Ruef was the only guest at the villa who had been to that country. I first suspected that someone was impersonating Prince Michael, but it turned out to be baseless. Then I thought Miss White might be an imposter, and when I remembered that Mrs. Ruef said she felt familiar with her, I could finally see through the clouds. It was clear that one thing was very important, that she could not be recognized by anyone, and Mrs. Ruef was the only one who might recognize her.\" [3264] \"Who is she?\" asked the Marquis of Cartonham.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHis appearance was all a facade. Lord Carterham mentioned that although he came here to see the first edition books, he never spoke when the topic was brought up. There are several suspicious things. There was light in his window on the night of the murder. And on the night of the incident in the conference hall, when I met him on the balcony, he was dressed up. The note could have been dropped by him, and you didn't see it was Mr. Kade who dropped it. Mr. Kade might have gone to Dover to investigate, and he might have been kidnapped there. I think Mr. Fuchs is much more suspicious than Mr. Kade.\" [2974] The Frenchman said sternly:\n[2975] \"From your point of view, maybe so, but I won't argue with you. I also agree that Mr.