ater, and Victor King was arrested in Paris. Everyone has been hoping that someday, we could find the diamond through him.\" [1445] \"But the Chimney House has been turned upside down many times.\" [1446] \"Yes.\" Batter spoke sharply, \"But if you don't know where to look, just searching aimlessly is useless. It's just a hypothesis now, that Victor King came here to find that thing and accidentally ran into Michael Prince. Then he killed him.\" [1447] \"That's possible.\" George said, \"That could very well be the truth of the case.\" [1448] \"I don't think it's that high a probability, just a certain possibility.\" [1449] \"Why?\" [1450] \"Victor King has never been known to kill anyone.\" Batter said solemnly. [1451] \"Oh, but he's a dangerous criminal...\"\n[1452] Batter shook his head discontentedly: \"Mr. Romax, the behavior of criminals has their own patterns, which is quite surprising. But...\"\n[1453] \"What?\" [1454] \"It's better to ask the servants of Prince Michael. I specifically left him until last. If you don't mind, please call him here.\" [1455] George agreed, and the detective rang the bell. Treadwell was summoned and left after receiving the instructions. [1456] After a while, he returned with a person. The person was tall, with blond hair, high cheekbones, and deep blue eyes. His face was expressionless, almost matching Battert's. [1457] \"Are you Boris Anchukov?\" [1458] \"Yes.\" [1459] \"Are you the personal servant of Michael Prince?\" [1460] \"Yes.\" He spoke English well, but with a distinct foreign accent. [1461] \"Do you know that your master was murdered last night?\" [1462] The man did not answer, but only let out a wild animal-like howl. George jumped back, hastily retreating towards the window. [1463] \"Where was the last time you saw your master?\" [1464] \"The prince retired at ten o'clock. As usual, I slept in the small room next to his. He must have gone through another door to the rooms downstairs, the one on the corridor. I didn