it,\" Anthony said thoughtfully, \"I believe it very much.\" [2264] \"Well,\" Bates said, \"he's right, the excitement of the evening is over. By the way, do you remember the dead man we found near Stanis?\" [2265] \"Yes, what about it?\" [2266] \"Nothing, his identity has been confirmed. His name is Gis\u00e8ppe Manelli, a waiter at the Hotel Britz. Isn't that strange?\" [2267] * * *\n[2268] [1] Edmond Rostand, a French author of detective novels (1864-1941), created the famous thief, adventurer, and detective Arsen\u00e8 Lupin. [2269] Chapter XX: Balthazar and Anthony's Agreement\n[2270] Anthony remained silent, gazing out the window. Balthazar watched his motionless back for a moment. [2271] \"Goodnight,\" he finally said, approaching the door. [2272] Anthony turned around. [2273] \"Wait, Balthazar,\" he said. [2274] Balthazar stopped in his tracks. Anthony left the window, took out a cigarette, lit it, and spoke: \"You seem quite interested in this matter of Stanislas.\" [2275] \"Not really, I just find it peculiar.\" [2276] \"Do you think it's the crime scene or do you think the body was moved?\" [2277] \"I think the victim was killed elsewhere and then transported here.\" [2278] \"Same thought,\" Anthony said. [2279] The detective noticed the emphasis in his words and looked at him intently. [2280] \"What do you think? Do you know who moved the body?\" [2281] \"I do,\" Anthony said. \"It was me.\" [2282] The lack of reaction from the other party made Anthony somewhat annoyed. [2283] \"Sheriff Butler, I must admit, you are quite composed about everything.\" [2284] \"Remaining calm is a principle I've learned works well for me.\" [2285] \"You've done an excellent job at that,\" Anthony said. \"I've never seen you lose your temper. So, do you want to hear the whole story?\" [2286] \"I'd like to hear the details,\" Butler said. [2287] Anthony pulled two chairs and they sat down. Then, Anthony recounted the events of the previous Thursday night in detail. [2288] Butler listened calmly throughout, and after Anthony finished, a faraway look came into his eyes. [2289] \"You know,\" he said, \"one of these days, you're going to get yourself into trouble.\" [2290] \"You mean I've escaped another brush with the law?\" [2291] \"We believe in the principle of not doing wrong repeatedly,\" said Chief Batter. [2292] \"That's a clever way of putting it,\" said Anthony, \"observing without revealing.\" [2293] \"What I can't understand is,\" said Batter, \"why you decided to tell me this now.\" [2294] \"How to explain it,\" said Anthony, \"Chief Batter, I have come to greatly appreciate your ability. You have always performed excellently in crucial moments, like tonight. I feel that if I withhold information, it would hinder your performance. You should know all the facts. I have done everything I can, but the situation is still a mess. Until tonight, I didn't say anything to protect Mrs. Rue. But now it has been proven that the letters had nothing to do with her, so the idea of her being an accomplice is absurd. I may have given her bad advice at the time, but she said she was just acting on impulse, so she gave the man money to keep it quiet, and I believed her.\" [2295] \"The jury won't believe it,\" said Batter, \"they have no imagination.\" [2296] \"So you just took her word for it?\" Anthony asked, curious. [2297] \"Mr. Kidd, you know, my work is dealing with the so-called upper class. Most people are always trying to guess what others are thinking, but two types never do: hobos and nobility. They do what they want, without caring about others' opinions. I'm not talking about idle rich who just go to parties, but those who are born with a disregard for others. In my view, they all look the same: fearless, honest, but sometimes incredibly stupid.\" [2298] \"An interesting speech,\" Anthony said. \"I think you should write a memoir; it would be worth reading.\" [2299] The sheriff smiled at the suggestion but didn't respond. [2300] \"I have a question for you,\" Anthony continued. \"Do you think there's a connection between me and Stanis? I can tell you have that idea from your attitude.\" [2301] \"Yes, I have a hunch, but there's no concrete evidence. Mr. Kidd, you've done a good job, meticulous.\" [2302] \"I'm glad to hear that.\" Anthony said. \"I have a feeling that since I met you, you've been setting small traps for me. Overall, I can avoid them, but I can still feel the pressure.\" [2303] Battert stubbornly smiled. [2304] \"That's the way to catch a big fish: let him go first, let him exhaust himself, and finally, he will be caught.\" [2305] \"You're a pleasant guy, Battert. I wonder when you plan to catch me?\" [2306] \"As I said before, 'many wrongs don't make a right.'\" [2307] \"Besides,\" Anthony said, \"I'm still just an amateur assistant?\" [2308] \"That's right.\" [2309] \"Your Watson to my Sherlock Holmes?\" [2310] \"Most detective novels are just made-up nonsense,\" Battert said expressionlessly, \"but they're entertaining to read. Sometimes, they can be useful too.\" [2311] \"How so?\" asked Anthony curiously. [2312] \"They make the reader generally think the police are fools. That's useful when you come across an amateurish crime, like a murder, for instance.\" [2313] Anthony looked at him silently for a few minutes, Batterton sitting motionless, occasionally blinking, with no expression on his placid face. After a while, he got up. [2314] \"There's no use going to bed now,\" he said. \"I'll have to talk to the Earl when he gets up. Everyone can leave if they want to, but if the Earl can invite the guests to stay on a personal basis, I'll be grateful. Sir, if you don't mind, please accept his invitation, and Mrs. Ruff.\" [2315] \"Did you find the gun?\" Anthony asked suddenly. [2316] \"Do you mean the gun that killed Prince Michael? No, not yet. However, it's definitely inside the villa or in the villa's courtyard. I got an idea from you and sent a few people to search the bird's nest. If we can get that gun, the case will have some leads. And that bundle of letters. You said there was a letter with the heading 'Smokehouse Villa', right? That must be his last letter, and it will definitely mention how to find the diamond.\" [2317] \"What do you think about Gisipp's death?\" [2318] \"He was a habitual thief and was probably found by either the Vicomte or the Red Hand, and then hired. It's not surprising if the Vicomte and the Red Hand work together. The Red Hand has money and power, but lacks wisdom. Gisipp's task was to steal the memoir, and they couldn't know that the letters were in your possession. By the way, that's quite a strange coincidence.\" [2319] \"I know,\" Anthony said. \"It's really quite amazing to think about it.\" [2320] \"Gisipp got those letters instead, and he was initially disappointed. After seeing the fragment of the letter cut out, he came up with the idea of blackmailing the lady. Of course, he didn't realize the true value of those letters. The Red Hand's comrades discovered his actions and thought he had deliberately betrayed them, so they killed him. They are fond of executing traitors, which is a unique characteristic for them. What I don't understand is the gun with the inscription 'Virginia'. Such a clever handling is not something the Red Hand could come up with. Normally, they like to post their red hand marks everywhere to serve as a warning to others. I think the Vicomte must have gotten involved here, but I don't understand his motives. It seems he wanted to frame Mrs. Ruff, but there's no special significance in doing so.\" [2321] \"I had an idea,\" Anthony said, \"but it didn't work as planned.\" [2322] He confessed to Batter that he had identified Virginia as Michael, and the police chief nodded. [2323] \"It's definitely Michael. By the way, the old baron was very impressed with you when you mentioned you would do your best to find the memoir by next Wednesday.\" [2324] \"He's a good man,\" Anthony said. \"I appreciate his kind words, especially since I told him I would do my best to find the memoir by next Wednesday.\" [2325] \"That's not an easy task,\" Batter said. [2326] \"Well, you think so too? I guess the Victorians have gotten their hands on those letters.\" [2327] Butler nodded. [2328] \"Taking the letters from Giuseppe on Pound Street was all part of the plan. They have them now and have deciphered the code, so they know where to look.\" [2329] The two were about to leave the room. [2330] \"Here?\" Anthony turned sharply. [2331] \"Yes, right here. But they haven't found them yet, so they'll take another risk to come back.\" [2332] \"I guess,\" Anthony said, \"you've already come up with a plan in that clever head of yours?\" [2333] Bartle didn't answer, looking particularly slow and dull-witted. Then, he slowly blinked. [2334] \"Do you need my help?\" Anthony asked. [2335] \"Yes, and another person's help.\" [2336] \"Who?\" [2337] \"Mrs. Reeve. You should know, she's a particularly deceptive woman.\" [2338] \"I've already figured that out,\" Anthony said. [2339] He glanced at his watch. [2340] \"Bartle, I agree with you. Go for a swim in the lake and have a hearty breakfast instead of sleeping. It's more meaningful.\" [2341] He lightly ran back to the room upstairs, whistling and shedding his nightclothes. Then he took out a morning robe and a towel. [2342] Suddenly, he stopped in front of the vanity, staring intently at something quietly placed in front of the mirror. [2343] For a moment, he couldn't believe his eyes, he picked up the item and examined it carefully. Yes, that's right. [2344] It was the bundle of letters signed by Virginia Reveaux, intact and unopened. [2345] Anthony held those letters, sinking into the chair. [2346] \"I can't make sense of it,\" he muttered to himself. \"I don't understand what's happening in this villa. How did these letters come back? Who put them on my vanity? Why?\" [2347] He couldn't find satisfactory answers to these crucial questions. [2348] Chapter 21: Mr. Eschaton's Suitcase\n[2349] At 10 a.m., the Earl of Chatham and his daughter were having breakfast, and Bandel looked worried. [2350] \"Father,\" she finally spoke. [2351] The Earl of Carrington was engrossed in reading The Times, and did not speak. [2352] \"Father,\" Bandel called out again, more sharply. [2353] The Earl of Carrington was engrossed in reading the news of an upcoming rare book auction, and only lifted his head when she called out to him. [2354] \"Ah?\" he said, \"Did you say something?\" [2355] \"Yes, who was that person eating breakfast over there?\" [2356] She indicated the clearly occupied seat with a nod of her head. All the other seats were empty. [2357] \"Ah, what was his name again?\" [2358] \"Fat Eddi?\" [2359] Bendlar and her father had a remarkable ability to understand each other without words, and they both seemed to grasp the meaning of the puzzling words. [2360] \"Yes, that's the one.\" [2361] \"I saw you talking to the sheriff before breakfast.\" [2362] The Earl of Carrington sighed. [2363] \"Yes, he caught me in the foyer. In my opinion, the time before breakfast is sacred. I have to go abroad; the pressure here is too much.\" [2364] Bandel abruptly interrupted him. [2365] \"What did he say?\" [2366] \"He said that anyone who wants to leave can leave.\" [2367] \"Oh,\" Bandel said, \"that's just what you wanted.\" [2368] \"I know. But he didn't finish. He went on to say that he still hopes I can invite everyone to stay.\" [2369] \"I don't understand,\" Bandel said, frowning. [2370] \"It's all so confusing, so contradictory,\" the Earl of Carrington complained, \"and he had to say it before breakfast.\" [2371] \"What did you say?\" [2372] \"Of course I agreed. There's no point arguing with this group, especially before breakfast.\" The Duke of Cambridge returned to his most vexing question. [2373] \"So who did you invite?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."